# Every sentence of RFC 9110 (rfc9110.txt, next to this file) that holds MUST,
# SHALL, REQUIRED, SHOULD or RECOMMENDED, and how shioko accounts for it.
#
#   @ <section>
#   > <the sentence>
#   = <status>      one or more; the kinds are described in tools/spec.sh,
#                   which also checks this file

@ 2.2
> The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
= info defines the requirement key words; not itself a requirement
> A sender MUST NOT generate protocol elements that do not match the grammar defined by the corresponding ABNF rules.
= code every message shioko generates itself (own responses, the 301, the ACME 200, the 100 Continue, the request line and fields written to the upstream) matches the ABNF of RFC 9110/9112, apart from a request target forwarded as received (see the deviation); test each against a strict reference parser
= test generated_messages_parse_strictly
= deviation a request target may hold characters the URI grammar forbids (| ^ [ ] { } ` " < > \), because browsers send them unencoded; it is forwarded to the upstream as received (PLAN 6, known departures)
= code on port 80, answer 400 instead of the 301 when the target is not in origin form, and in the Location percent-encode every octet a URI path or query may not hold and every "%" that does not begin a valid escape, so the Location written is always a valid URI reference
= test a_site_with_a_certificate_is_redirected_to_https
> Within a given message, a sender MUST NOT generate protocol elements or syntax alternatives that are only allowed to be generated by participants in other roles (i.e., a role that the sender does not have for that message).
= code generate only server-role syntax in own responses and only client-role syntax in the request sent upstream: no obs-fold, no response-only fields in the request, no request-only fields in own responses
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test an_error

@ 2.3
> A recipient SHOULD parse a received protocol element defensively, with only marginal expectations that the element will conform to its ABNF grammar and fit within a reasonable buffer size.
= code parse every head through the bounds-checked cursor without assuming it conforms to the grammar; answer a request line over 8 KB with 414 and a request head over 16 KB or 100 fields with 431, and bound the upstream's response head the same way (502)
= test upstream_head_limits
= test request_head_limits
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> At a minimum, a recipient MUST be able to parse and process protocol element lengths that are at least as long as the values that it generates for those same protocol elements in other messages.
= code accept from clients and upstreams elements at least as long as shioko emits itself: chunk sizes and Content-Length over the full u64 range, a request target as long as the one it forwards or puts into Location
= test chunk_sizes
= test content_length_classes

@ 2.4
> A recipient MUST interpret a received protocol element according to the semantics defined for it by this specification, including extensions to this specification, unless the recipient has determined (through experience or configuration) that the sender incorrectly implements what is implied by those semantics.
= code interpret Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding, Connection, Host, Expect and Upgrade as RFC 9110/9112 define them, with no peer-specific workarounds; of the other fields only Via is read (for loops), and only the forwarding fields are rewritten (X-Real-IP, X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto, Via); the rest are forwarded uninterpreted
= test end_to_end_fields_are_passed_on_unchanged
= test content_length_classes
= test transfer_encoding_classes
= test lists
= test hosts
= test continue_only_for_a_body
= test which_requests_are_upgrades

@ 3.3
> As a result, a server MUST NOT assume that two requests on the same connection are from the same user agent unless the connection is secured and specific to that agent.
= row step relay keep plain done:- => idle close plain read_head:-
= code keep no state from one request to the next on a connection: every request head is checked on its own (Host against the SNI site) and gets its own new upstream connection
= test every_request_on_a_tls_connection_is_checked
= test an_upstream_is_closed_when_its_exchange_ends
= test request_intact
= test head_at_boundary

@ 4.1
> It is RECOMMENDED that all senders and recipients support, at a minimum, URIs with lengths of 8000 octets in protocol elements.
= row step idle close plain fail:414 => closed close plain error:414
= code the 8 KB request-line limit is 8192 octets: a request line with an 8000-octet target is accepted, forwarded whole and copied whole into the 301 Location; only a longer line gets 414
= test a_long_target_is_forwarded_whole
= test a_site_with_a_certificate_is_redirected_to_https
= test request_head_limits

@ 4.2.1
> A sender MUST NOT generate an "http" URI with an empty host identifier.
= unused generating "http" URIs: the only URI shioko generates is the "https" Location of the 301
> A recipient that processes such a URI reference MUST reject it as invalid.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code refuse with 400 a Host whose name is empty (empty value, or only ":port") and every absolute-form request target, so no "http" URI with an empty host is ever processed
= test hosts
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why

@ 4.2.2
> A sender MUST NOT generate an "https" URI with an empty host identifier.
= code write the Location of the 301 as "https://", the site's configured name and the request target percent-encoded where a URI may not hold it; the host is never taken from the request and never empty
= test a_site_with_a_certificate_is_redirected_to_https
> A recipient that processes such a URI reference MUST reject it as invalid.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code refuse with 400 a Host whose name is empty and every absolute-form request target, so no "https" URI with an empty host is ever processed
= test hosts
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> A client MUST ensure that its HTTP requests for an "https" resource are secured, prior to being communicated, and that it only accepts secured responses to those requests.
= peer user agent: nothing to do on the client-facing side; a site with a cert is never served on port 80, it only gets the 301
= deviation the upstream hop is plain HTTP, also for "https" requests (PLAN 6, known departures; PLAN 2 drops HTTPS upstreams)

@ 4.2.3
> As a result, distinct resources SHOULD NOT be identified by HTTP URIs that are equivalent after normalization (using any method defined in Section 6.2 of [URI]).
= peer origin server (resource owner): nothing to do; shioko never normalises or compares paths, the target goes to the upstream unchanged
= code compare the name part of Host with the site's name ignoring case
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test on_a_tls_connection_the_host_must_be_the_site_of_the_handshake

@ 4.2.4
> A sender MUST NOT generate the userinfo subcomponent (and its "@" delimiter) when an "http" or "https" URI reference is generated within a message as a target URI or field value.
= code the Location of the 301 is "https://", the site's configured name and a target that starts with "/", so its authority never has a userinfo part; a target not in origin-form is refused with 400 before any redirect
= test a_site_with_a_certificate_is_redirected_to_https
> Before making use of an "http" or "https" URI reference received from an untrusted source, a recipient SHOULD parse for userinfo and treat its presence as an error; it is likely being used to obscure the authority for the sake of phishing attacks.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code refuse with 400 a Host that is not name or name:port (so one containing "@") and every absolute-form target; no URI with userinfo is used for routing or for the 301
= test hosts
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why

@ 4.3.4
> To establish a secured connection to dereference a URI, a client MUST verify that the service's identity is an acceptable match for the URI's origin server.
= unused HTTPS towards the upstream: shioko never opens a secured connection as a client (PLAN 2 drops HTTPS upstreams), so it verifies no certificate
> In general, a client MUST verify the service identity using the verification process defined in Section 6 of [RFC6125].
= unused HTTPS towards the upstream: shioko never opens a secured connection as a client (PLAN 2 drops HTTPS upstreams), so it verifies no certificate
> The client MUST construct a reference identity from the service's host: if the host is a literal IP address (Section 4.3.5), the reference identity is an IP-ID, otherwise the host is a name and the reference identity is a DNS-ID.
= unused HTTPS towards the upstream: shioko never opens a secured connection as a client (PLAN 2 drops HTTPS upstreams), so it verifies no certificate
> A reference identity of type CN-ID MUST NOT be used by clients.
= unused HTTPS towards the upstream: shioko never opens a secured connection as a client (PLAN 2 drops HTTPS upstreams), so it verifies no certificate
> If the certificate is not valid for the target URI's origin, a user agent MUST either obtain confirmation from the user before proceeding (see Section 3.5) or terminate the connection with a bad certificate error.
= peer user agent: nothing to do; a client that rejects our certificate sends an alert or closes, which ends the connection
> Automated clients MUST log the error to an appropriate audit log (if available) and SHOULD terminate the connection (with a bad certificate error).
= unused HTTPS towards the upstream: shioko never opens a secured connection as a client (PLAN 2 drops HTTPS upstreams), so it verifies no certificate
> Automated clients MAY provide a configuration setting that disables this check, but MUST provide a setting which enables it.
= unused HTTPS towards the upstream: shioko never opens a secured connection as a client (PLAN 2 drops HTTPS upstreams), so it verifies no certificate

@ 5.1
> A proxy MUST forward unrecognized header fields unless the field name is listed in the Connection header field (Section 7.6.1) or the proxy is specifically configured to block, or otherwise transform, such fields.
= peer forward proxy: nothing to do
= code forward every request and response header field shioko does not recognise, name and value unchanged, unless Connection names it
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
> Other recipients SHOULD ignore unrecognized header and trailer fields.
= code never refuse a message or change behaviour because of a header field shioko does not recognise
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us

@ 5.3
> The order in which field lines with the same name are received is therefore significant to the interpretation of the field value; a proxy MUST NOT change the order of these field line values when forwarding a message.
= peer forward proxy: nothing to do
= code keep field lines of the same name in the received order when writing a head, never reorder their values; shioko's own X-Forwarded-For and Via members go after the client's
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
> This means that, aside from the well-known exception noted below, a sender MUST NOT generate multiple field lines with the same name in a message (whether in the headers or trailers) or append a field line when a field line of the same name already exists in the message, unless that field's definition allows multiple field line values to be recombined as a comma-separated list (i.e., at least one alternative of the field's definition allows a comma-separated list, such as an ABNF rule of #(values) defined in Section 5.6.1).
= code write exactly one Host, X-Real-IP, X-Forwarded-Proto, Connection and body-length field to the upstream: the client's X-Real-IP and X-Forwarded-Proto are dropped, its Content-Length is not copied, and shioko's X-Forwarded-For and Via values are appended as list members to the client's
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
> A server MUST NOT apply a request to the target resource until it receives the entire request header section, since later header field lines might include conditionals, authentication credentials, or deliberately misleading duplicate header fields that could impact request processing.
= row step idle close plain request:close => connect close plain connect:-
= row step idle close plain request:keep => connect keep plain connect:-
= test refused_never_forwarded

@ 5.4
> A server that receives a request header field line, field value, or set of fields larger than it wishes to process MUST respond with an appropriate 4xx (Client Error) status code.
= row step idle close plain fail:431 => closed close plain error:431
= row step idle close plain fail:414 => closed close plain error:414
= code answer a request line over 8 KB with 414 and a request head over 16 KB or 100 fields with 431, then close
= test request_head_limits
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why

@ 5.5
> When a specific version of HTTP allows such whitespace to appear in a message, a field parsing implementation MUST exclude such whitespace prior to evaluating the field value.
= code strip leading and trailing SP and HTAB from every field line value before evaluating it ("Content-Length:  42 " is 42) and do not re-emit them
= test field_lines
> Specifications for newly defined fields SHOULD limit their values to visible US-ASCII octets (VCHAR), SP, and HTAB.
= info requirement on specifications of new fields; the values shioko writes itself (X-Forwarded-*, X-Real-IP) are visible ASCII
> A recipient SHOULD treat other allowed octets in field content (i.e., obs-text) as opaque data.
= code accept octets 0x80-0xFF in field values and forward them unchanged, without decoding or validating them as any charset
= test octets_pass_through_as_they_are
= test field_lines
> Field values containing CR, LF, or NUL characters are invalid and dangerous, due to the varying ways that implementations might parse and interpret those characters; a recipient of CR, LF, or NUL within a field value MUST either reject the message or replace each of those characters with SP before further processing or forwarding of that message.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= code refuse a head that has CR, LF or NUL inside a field value (bare CR, bare LF, obs-fold): 400 for a request head, 502 for an upstream response head; never replace and forward
= test bare_cr_from_upstreams
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse

@ 5.6.1.1
> In any production that uses the list construct, a sender MUST NOT generate empty list elements.
= code never emit an empty list element in a field shioko writes (Connection, Transfer-Encoding, X-Forwarded-For, Via): no leading, trailing or doubled commas, also when the client's X-Forwarded-For or Via was empty
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test forwarded_for_has_no_empty_elements

@ 5.6.1.2
> A recipient MUST parse and ignore a reasonable number of empty list elements: enough to handle common mistakes by senders that merge values, but not so much that they could be used as a denial-of-service mechanism.
= code when parsing Connection, Upgrade and Expect skip empty list elements: "close, , upgrade," names close and upgrade; their number is bounded by the 16 KB head limit
= test continue_only_for_a_body
= test which_requests_are_upgrades
= test lists
= deviation a Transfer-Encoding or Content-Length list with empty elements ("chunked," or ", chunked") is refused, although recipients must accept such lists (PLAN 6, known departures)
> In other words, a recipient MUST accept lists that satisfy the following syntax:
= code accept "[ element ] *( OWS "," OWS [ element ] )" for Connection, Upgrade and Expect
= test continue_only_for_a_body
= test which_requests_are_upgrades
= test lists
= deviation a Transfer-Encoding or Content-Length list with empty elements is refused, although recipients must accept such lists (PLAN 6, known departures)

@ 5.6.3
> For protocol elements where optional whitespace is preferred to improve readability, a sender SHOULD generate the optional whitespace as a single SP; otherwise, a sender SHOULD NOT generate optional whitespace except as needed to overwrite invalid or unwanted protocol elements during in-place message filtering.
= code write each generated field line as name, colon, one SP, value, and separate generated list members with comma SP; no other optional whitespace
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test an_error
> A sender SHOULD generate RWS as a single SP.
= code where a generated field value needs RWS write exactly one SP (only a Via member would need it)
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
> A sender MUST NOT generate BWS in messages.
= code generate no BWS: no whitespace before the colon of a field line, around "=" in a parameter, or inside chunk-size lines
= test chunks_are_decoded_and_the_rest_left_alone
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
> A recipient MUST parse for such bad whitespace and remove it before interpreting the protocol element.
= code where the grammar has BWS (around ";" and "=" in chunk extensions) accept SP and HTAB and discard them; never forward them
= test chunk_sizes
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped

@ 5.6.4
> Recipients that process the value of a quoted-string MUST handle a quoted-pair as if it were replaced by the octet following the backslash.
= unused interpreting quoted-string values: shioko interprets no field whose value has quoted-string syntax; such values are forwarded as opaque octets, quoted-pairs included
> A sender SHOULD NOT generate a quoted-pair in a quoted-string except where necessary to quote DQUOTE and backslash octets occurring within that string.
= unused generating quoted-strings: shioko generates none; received ones are forwarded unchanged
> A sender SHOULD NOT generate a quoted-pair in a comment except where necessary to quote parentheses ["(" and ")"] and backslash octets occurring within that comment.
= unused generating comments: shioko generates none; received ones are forwarded unchanged

@ 5.6.7
> A recipient that parses a timestamp value in an HTTP field MUST accept all three HTTP-date formats.
= unused parsing HTTP-date: shioko interprets no date field (it is not a cache); received dates are forwarded unchanged
> When a sender generates a field that contains one or more timestamps defined as HTTP-date, the sender MUST generate those timestamps in the IMF-fixdate format.
= code write the Date of own responses, and the Date added to an upstream response that had none, in IMF-fixdate, e.g. "Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT"
= test dates
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
> A sender MUST NOT generate additional whitespace in an HTTP-date beyond that specifically included as SP in the grammar.
= code a generated HTTP-date has exactly the single SPs of the IMF-fixdate grammar, with zero-padded two-digit day, hour, minute and second
= test dates
> Recipients of a timestamp value in rfc850-date format, which uses a two-digit year, MUST interpret a timestamp that appears to be more than 50 years in the future as representing the most recent year in the past that had the same last two digits.
= unused parsing HTTP-date: shioko interprets no date field; received dates are forwarded unchanged

@ 6
> However, a client MUST retain knowledge of the request when parsing, interpreting, or caching a corresponding response.
= row response_framing 1.1 bodiless one none => empty
= row step response keep upgrade switching:- => tunnel keep upgrade tunnel:-
= row step response keep plain switching:- => closed close plain error:502
= code keep the request's method, version and upgrade flag until its response head is read: a response to HEAD is framed as bodiless whatever its Content-Length says
= test response_intact
= test a_101_nobody_asked_for_is_a_bad_gateway

@ 6.2
> A client SHOULD send a request version equal to the highest version to which the client is conformant and whose major version is no higher than the highest version supported by the server, if this is known.
= code write "HTTP/1.1" in the request line sent to the upstream, whatever version the client used
= test request_intact
> A client MUST NOT send a version to which it is not conformant.
= code never copy the client's version token to the upstream; the forwarded request line always says HTTP/1.1
= test request_intact
> A server SHOULD send a response version equal to the highest version to which the server is conformant that has a major version less than or equal to the one received in the request.
= code write "HTTP/1.1" in every status line sent to the client, for own and for relayed responses, also when the client or the upstream spoke HTTP/1.0
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
= test response_intact
> A server MUST NOT send a version to which it is not conformant.
= code never copy the upstream's version token into the status line sent to the client
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
> A recipient that receives a message with a major version number that it implements and a minor version number higher than what it implements SHOULD process the message as if it were in the highest minor version within that major version to which the recipient is conformant.
= code treat a request or an upstream response marked HTTP/1.x with x > 1 as HTTP/1.1 (PLAN 6: a higher minor version is treated as 1.1)
= test unknown_status_codes_are_relayed
= test request_lines

@ 6.5.1
> A sender MUST NOT generate a trailer field unless the sender knows the corresponding header field name's definition permits the field to be sent in trailers.
= unused generating trailer fields: shioko originates none, and received trailers are read and dropped in both directions (PLAN 6), so a chunked body it writes ends with an empty trailer section
> A recipient MUST NOT merge a trailer field into a header section unless the recipient understands the corresponding header field definition and that definition explicitly permits and defines how trailer field values can be safely merged.
= code read the trailer section of a chunked request or response and drop it; never move a trailer field into the header section of the forwarded message
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped
> Because of the potential for trailer fields to be discarded in transit, a server SHOULD NOT generate trailer fields that it believes are necessary for the user agent to receive.
= unused generating trailer fields: shioko originates none

@ 6.5.2
> Trailer fields that might be generated more than once during a message MUST be defined as a list-based field even if each member value is only processed once per field line received.
= info requirement on the definitions of trailer fields, not on implementations

@ 6.6.1
> A sender that generates a Date header field SHOULD generate its field value as the best available approximation of the date and time of message generation.
= code take the value of a generated Date from the system clock at the time the response head is written
= test every_response_is_dated_when_it_is_written
> An origin server with a clock (as defined in Section 5.6.7) MUST generate a Date header field in all 2xx (Successful), 3xx (Redirection), and 4xx (Client Error) responses, and MAY generate a Date header field in 1xx (Informational) and 5xx (Server Error) responses.
= code send Date in every own response: 400, 403, 404, 413, 414, 421, 431, 501, 502, 504 and the port-80 301
= test an_error
= code write Date on every response shioko originates: the errors, the 301 and the ACME token 200
= test an_error
> An origin server without a clock MUST NOT generate a Date header field.
= unused running without a clock: the hosts have a system clock and shioko always generates Date
> A recipient with a clock that receives a response message without a Date header field MUST record the time it was received and append a corresponding Date header field to the message's header section if it is cached or forwarded downstream.
= code when the upstream's response head has no Date, add one with the time the head was received before writing the head to the client
= test every_response_is_dated_when_it_is_written
= test interim_and_bodiless_responses_carry_no_framing
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us

@ 6.6.2
> A sender that intends to generate one or more trailer fields in a message SHOULD generate a Trailer header field in the header section of that message to indicate which fields might be present in the trailers.
= unused generating trailer fields: shioko originates none, so it generates no Trailer field

@ 7.1
> These forms MUST NOT be used with other methods.
= peer client: a target that is not origin-form, "*" with a method other than OPTIONS, and any CONNECT are refused with 400
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400

@ 7.2
> A user agent MUST generate a Host header field in a request unless it sends that information as an ":authority" pseudo-header field.
= peer user agent: a request with no Host field, or more than one, is refused with 400
= code send exactly one Host field in every request to the upstream
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
> A user agent that sends Host SHOULD send it as the first field in the header section of a request.
= peer user agent: nothing to do; Host is accepted at any position in the header section (shioko, not being a user agent, writes Host after the client's fields, PLAN 6)

@ 7.4
> Unless the connection is from a trusted gateway, an origin server MUST reject a request if any scheme-specific requirements for the target URI are not met.
= row step idle close plain fail:421 => closed close plain error:421
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code answer 421 on port 443 when the Host name is not the site selected by SNI and 400 for an absolute-form target; on port 80 never forward a request for a site that has a cert (301) or for an unknown host (404)
= test on_a_tls_connection_the_host_must_be_the_site_of_the_handshake
= test a_site_with_a_certificate_is_redirected_to_https
= test refused_never_forwarded
> In particular, a request for an "https" resource MUST be rejected unless it has been received over a connection that has been secured via a certificate valid for that target URI's origin, as defined by Section 4.2.2.
= row step idle close plain fail:421 => closed close plain error:421
= code on port 80 a request for a site with a cert is answered with 301 and never forwarded; on port 443 the Host name must be the SNI site whose certificate was presented; an absolute-form "https://" target is refused with 400 on either port
= test a_site_with_a_certificate_is_redirected_to_https
= test on_a_tls_connection_the_host_must_be_the_site_of_the_handshake
= test request_lines

@ 7.5
> A client that receives a response while it is still sending the associated request SHOULD continue sending that request unless it receives an explicit indication to the contrary (see, e.g., Section 9.5 of [HTTP/1.1] and Section 6.4 of [HTTP/2]).
= test response_after_request
= row step forward keep plain sent:- => response keep plain read_response:-

@ 7.6
> An intermediary not acting as a tunnel MUST implement the Connection header field, as specified in Section 7.6.1, and exclude fields from being forwarded that are only intended for the incoming connection.
= code leave out of the forwarded request and of the forwarded response head every field named in Connection plus Connection, Keep-Alive, Proxy-Connection, TE, Transfer-Encoding, Upgrade and Expect, and write shioko's own Connection field
= test end_to_end_fields_are_passed_on_unchanged
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
> An intermediary MUST NOT forward a message to itself unless it is protected from an infinite request loop.
= code refuse at startup a site whose upstream is a loopback address at port 80 or 443
= test every_mistake_is_an_error_with_its_line
= code refuse at startup an upstream on a loopback address at port 80 or 443, and answer 400 to a request whose Via already names shioko 8 times
= test every_mistake_is_an_error_with_its_line
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why

@ 7.6.1
> When a field aside from Connection is used to supply control information for or about the current connection, the sender MUST list the corresponding field name within the Connection header field.
= code list in shioko's own Connection field every connection-specific field it sends: "upgrade" with the "Upgrade: websocket" sent to the upstream; send no Keep-Alive or TE field of its own
= test tunnel_rules
= test request_intact
> Intermediaries MUST parse a received Connection header field before a message is forwarded and, for each connection-option in this field, remove any header or trailer field(s) from the message with the same name as the connection-option, and then remove the Connection header field itself (or replace it with the intermediary's own control options for the forwarded message).
= code parse Connection as a case-insensitive token list before writing the forwarded head, in both directions, leave out every field with a listed name, and replace Connection with shioko's own; trailer fields are dropped altogether
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped
> Furthermore, intermediaries SHOULD remove or replace fields that are known to require removal before forwarding, whether or not they appear as a connection-option, after applying those fields' semantics.
= code leave Proxy-Connection, Keep-Alive, TE, Transfer-Encoding, Upgrade and Expect out of a forwarded head even when Connection does not name them; framing and, for an allowed WebSocket upgrade, "Upgrade: websocket" are written by shioko itself
= test end_to_end_fields_are_passed_on_unchanged
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
> A sender MUST NOT send a connection option corresponding to a field that is intended for all recipients of the content.
= code shioko's own Connection field lists only close, keep-alive or upgrade, never the name of an end-to-end field
= test request_intact
= test an_error
= peer client: a field the client names in Connection is left out of the forwarded request; Host, the body length, X-Real-IP, X-Forwarded-*, Via and Connection are then written by shioko from what it parsed, so naming them does not change routing or framing

@ 7.6.2
> Each intermediary that receives a TRACE or OPTIONS request containing a Max-Forwards header field MUST check and update its value prior to forwarding the request.
= deviation Max-Forwards is not processed for TRACE and OPTIONS; like nginx, shioko presents itself as the origin and forwards the field unchanged (PLAN 6, known departures)
> If the received value is zero (0), the intermediary MUST NOT forward the request; instead, the intermediary MUST respond as the final recipient.
= deviation Max-Forwards is not processed for TRACE and OPTIONS; like nginx, shioko presents itself as the origin and forwards the field unchanged (PLAN 6, known departures)
> If the received Max-Forwards value is greater than zero, the intermediary MUST generate an updated Max-Forwards field in the forwarded message with a field value that is the lesser of a) the received value decremented by one (1) or b) the recipient's maximum supported value for Max-Forwards.
= deviation Max-Forwards is not processed for TRACE and OPTIONS; like nginx, shioko presents itself as the origin and forwards the field unchanged (PLAN 6, known departures)

@ 7.6.3
> A proxy MUST send an appropriate Via header field, as described below, in each message that it forwards.
= peer forward proxy: nothing to do
> An HTTP-to-HTTP gateway MUST send an appropriate Via header field in each inbound request message and MAY send a Via header field in forwarded response messages.
= code append shioko's own member to the Via of every request written to the upstream, as a Via field line after the client's (a new Via field if the client sent none): "1.1 shioko", or "1.0 shioko" for an HTTP/1.0 request
= test request_intact
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= code append a Via member made of the client's HTTP version and "shioko" ("1.0 shioko" or "1.1 shioko") to every forwarded request
= test request_intact
> An intermediary used as a portal through a network firewall SHOULD NOT forward the names and ports of hosts within the firewall region unless it is explicitly enabled to do so.
= code the upstream address from the config never appears in a field or body that shioko generates; its own Via member uses the pseudonym "shioko"
= test request_intact
= test an_error
= deviation a Via in an upstream response is passed to the client unchanged (PLAN 6, known departures)
> If not enabled, such an intermediary SHOULD replace each received-by host of any host behind the firewall by an appropriate pseudonym for that host.
= deviation a Via in an upstream response is passed to the client unchanged (PLAN 6, known departures)
> A sender SHOULD NOT combine multiple list members unless they are all under the same organizational control and the hosts have already been replaced by pseudonyms.
= unused combining Via members: shioko only appends its own member and never collapses received ones
> A sender MUST NOT combine members that have different received-protocol values.
= unused combining Via members: shioko only appends its own member and never collapses received ones

@ 7.7
> A proxy MUST NOT change the host name if the target URI contains a fully qualified domain name.
= peer forward proxy: nothing to do
= code send the Host value received from the client to the upstream unchanged
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
> A proxy MUST NOT modify the "absolute-path" and "query" parts of the received target URI when forwarding it to the next inbound server except as required by that forwarding protocol.
= peer forward proxy: nothing to do
= code forward the request target to the upstream octet for octet (PLAN 6: never inspected, rewritten or normalised)
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test request_intact
> A proxy MUST NOT transform the content (Section 6.4) of a response message that contains a no-transform cache directive (Section 5.2.2.6 of [CACHING]).
= peer forward proxy: nothing to do
= code relay content octets unchanged in both directions; only the transfer coding (chunked) is added or removed, which the RFC does not count as transforming
= test request_intact
= test response_intact
> A proxy SHOULD NOT modify header fields that provide information about the endpoints of the communication chain, the resource state, or the selected representation (other than the content) unless the field's definition specifically allows such modification or the modification is deemed necessary for privacy or security.
= peer forward proxy: nothing to do
= code write every end-to-end field of a forwarded head with its value unchanged; shioko leaves out only hop-by-hop fields and the client's X-Real-IP and X-Forwarded-Proto, appends to X-Forwarded-For and Via, and adds Date to a response that has none
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us

@ 7.8
> Although protocol names are registered with a preferred case, recipients SHOULD use case-insensitive comparison when matching each protocol-name to supported protocols.
= code compare protocol names in Upgrade ("websocket") and connection options ("upgrade") case-insensitively
= test which_requests_are_upgrades
= test tunnel_rules
= test a_websocket_tunnel_carries_bytes_both_ways_and_ends_as_one
> A server that sends a 101 (Switching Protocols) response MUST send an Upgrade header field to indicate the new protocol(s) to which the connection is being switched; if multiple protocol layers are being switched, the sender MUST list the protocols in layer-ascending order.
= row step response keep upgrade fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= code write the 101 to the client with "Connection: upgrade" and "Upgrade: websocket" (PLAN 6), and only when the upstream's 101 lists websocket in Upgrade and upgrade in Connection; any other 101 is answered with 502
= test tunnel_rules
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse
> A server MUST NOT switch to a protocol that was not indicated by the client in the corresponding request's Upgrade header field.
= row step response keep upgrade switching:- => tunnel keep upgrade tunnel:-
= row step response keep plain switching:- => closed close plain error:502
= row step response keep upgrade fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= check Http.TunnelOnlyOnUpgrade
= test tunnel_rules
= code treat a request as an upgrade only when it is an HTTP/1.1 GET without a body that lists websocket in Upgrade on a site with websocket = yes, send only "Upgrade: websocket" to the upstream, and answer a 101 that does not carry Upgrade: websocket with 502
= test which_requests_are_upgrades
= test what_is_not_a_websocket_upgrade_is_not_forwarded_as_one
= test tunnel_rules
> A server that sends a 426 (Upgrade Required) response MUST send an Upgrade header field to indicate the acceptable protocols, in order of descending preference.
= deviation a relayed 426 loses its Upgrade field like any hop-by-hop field (PLAN 6, known departures)
> A server MUST NOT switch protocols unless the received message semantics can be honored by the new protocol; an OPTIONS request can be honored by any protocol.
= peer origin server (upstream): nothing to do; shioko does not decide to switch, it relays the upstream's 101 and tunnels
> A sender of Upgrade MUST also send an "Upgrade" connection option in the Connection header field (Section 7.6.1) to inform intermediaries not to forward this field.
= code write "Connection: upgrade" together with "Upgrade: websocket" in the upgrade request sent to the upstream; in every other forwarded message the Upgrade field is left out
= test tunnel_rules
= test what_is_not_a_websocket_upgrade_is_not_forwarded_as_one
> A server that receives an Upgrade header field in an HTTP/1.0 request MUST ignore that Upgrade field.
= code an HTTP/1.0 request with Upgrade is never an upgrade: the field is left out and the request served as a plain one (PLAN 6: only an HTTP/1.1 GET without a body; Http.tla RequestHeads: upgrade implies version 1.1)
= test what_is_not_a_websocket_upgrade_is_not_forwarded_as_one
> If a server receives both an Upgrade and an Expect header field with the "100-continue" expectation (Section 10.1.1), the server MUST send a 100 (Continue) response before sending a 101 (Switching Protocols) response.
= code never treat a request that carries Expect: 100-continue as an upgrade request, so no 101 can follow such a request
= test what_is_not_a_websocket_upgrade_is_not_forwarded_as_one

