= test responses_without_a_body
> A server MUST NOT send a Content-Length header field in any 2xx (Successful) response to a CONNECT request (Section 9.3.6).
= unused CONNECT: every CONNECT request is answered with 400 (PLAN 6), so shioko never forwards one and never sends or receives a 2xx answer to one
> Aside from the cases defined above, in the absence of Transfer- Encoding, an origin server SHOULD send a Content-Length header field when the content size is known prior to sending the complete header section.
= code send Content-Length equal to the body length in every own response (PLAN 6), the ACME token included; for HEAD the same value without the body
= test an_error
= test a_response_to_head_has_no_body_but_states_its_length
= test acme_tokens_are_served_from_the_directory_for_any_host
> Since there is no predefined limit to the length of content, a recipient MUST anticipate potentially large decimal numerals and prevent parsing errors due to integer conversion overflows or precision loss due to integer conversion (Section 17.5).
= row request_framing 1.1 bad none => reject
= row response_framing 1.1 body bad none => reject
= code parse Content-Length digit by digit with checked u64 arithmetic; a value that overflows counts as "bad" (400 from a client, 502 from the upstream), never wrapped or truncated
= test content_length_classes
= test decimal_is_exact
> As a result, a sender MUST NOT forward a message with a Content- Length header field value that is known to be incorrect.
= check Http.AmbiguousRejected
= row step relay keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain
